<h3 class="text-text-100 mt-3 -mb-1 text-[1.125rem] font-bold">📊 Metrics You Can Defend</h3>
<div class="overflow-x-auto w-full px-2 mb-6"><table class="min-w-full border-collapse text-sm leading-[1.7] whitespace-normal"><thead class="text-left"><tr><th scope="col" class="text-text-100 border-b-0.5 border-[hsl(var(--border-300)/0.6)] py-2 pr-4 align-top font-bold">Metric</th><th scope="col" class="text-text-100 border-b-0.5 border-[hsl(var(--border-300)/0.6)] py-2 pr-4 align-top font-bold">Value</th><th scope="col" class="text-text-100 border-b-0.5 border-[hsl(var(--border-300)/0.6)] py-2 pr-4 align-top font-bold">How to defend it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="border-b-0.5 border-[hsl(var(--border-300)/0.3)] py-2 pr-4 align-top">ROC-AUC</td><td class="border-b-0.5 border-[hsl(var(--border-300)/0.3)] py-2 pr-4 align-top">0.9850</td><td class="border-b-0.5 border-[hsl(var(--border-300)/0.3)] py-2 pr-4 align-top">Measured on held-out test set of 56,962 transactions</td></tr><tr><td class="border-b-0.5 border-[hsl(var(--border-300)/0.3)] py-2 pr-4 align-top">Fraud Recall</td><td class="border-b-0.5 border-[hsl(var(--border-300)/0.3)] py-2 pr-4 align-top">85.33%</td><td class="border-b-0.5 border-[hsl(var(--border-300)/0.3)] py-2 pr-4 align-top">Caught 64 out of 75 fraud cases</td></tr><tr><td class="border-b-0.5 border-[hsl(var(--border-300)/0.3)] py-2 pr-4 align-top">Inference latency</td><td class="border-b-0.5 border-[hsl(var(--border-300)/0.3)] py-2 pr-4 align-top">4–10ms</td><td class="border-b-0.5 border-[hsl(var(--border-300)/0.3)] py-2 pr-4 align-top">Measured per request in logs</td></tr><tr><td class="border-b-0.5 border-[hsl(var(--border-300)/0.3)] py-2 pr-4 align-top">Throughput</td><td class="border-b-0.5 border-[hsl(var(--border-300)/0.3)] py-2 pr-4 align-top">6,448 req/min</td><td class="border-b-0.5 border-[hsl(var(--border-300)/0.3)] py-2 pr-4 align-top">Load tested with 10 concurrent threads</td></tr><tr><td class="border-b-0.5 border-[hsl(var(--border-300)/0.3)] py-2 pr-4 align-top">Error rate</td><td class="border-b-0.5 border-[hsl(var(--border-300)/0.3)] py-2 pr-4 align-top">0%</td><td class="border-b-0.5 border-[hsl(var(--border-300)/0.3)] py-2 pr-4 align-top">Zero crashes under concurrent load</td></tr><tr><td class="border-b-0.5 border-[hsl(var(--border-300)/0.3)] py-2 pr-4 align-top">Rate block rate</td><td class="border-b-0.5 border-[hsl(var(--border-300)/0.3)] py-2 pr-4 align-top">52%+</td><td class="border-b-0.5 border-[hsl(var(--border-300)/0.3)] py-2 pr-4 align-top">Verified via Redis sliding window</td></tr><tr><td class="border-b-0.5 border-[hsl(var(--border-300)/0.3)] py-2 pr-4 align-top">Fraud rings detected</td><td class="border-b-0.5 border-[hsl(var(--border-300)/0.3)] py-2 pr-4 align-top">Real time</td><td class="border-b-0.5 border-[hsl(var(--border-300)/0.3)] py-2 pr-4 align-top">Graph builds dynamically per transaction</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<hr class="border-border-200 border-t-0.5 my-3 mx-1.5">
<h3 class="text-text-100 mt-3 -mb-1 text-[1.125rem] font-bold">🔥 Top 15 Interview Questions + Perfect Answers</h3>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal"><strong>Q1: Tell me about your fraud detection project.</strong></p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal">"I built an end-to-end fraud detection platform with four layers — rate limiting, ML scoring, graph detection, and explainability. Each layer solves a different problem: rate limiting stops abuse before ML runs, ML catches individual transaction anomalies, graph detection finds fraud rings across multiple transactions, and explainability makes decisions auditable."</p>
<hr class="border-border-200 border-t-0.5 my-3 mx-1.5">
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal"><strong>Q2: Why Gradient Boosting and not deep learning?</strong></p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal">"Gradient Boosting performs extremely well on tabular data because it captures non-linear feature interactions without requiring feature scaling. Deep learning needs much more data and compute to outperform it on structured fraud datasets. I chose it for performance, interpretability, and production reliability."</p>
<hr class="border-border-200 border-t-0.5 my-3 mx-1.5">
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal"><strong>Q3: How did you handle class imbalance?</strong></p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal">"The dataset has only 0.17% fraud — 492 out of 284,807 transactions. I applied sample weights giving fraud cases 545x more importance during training. I also used a threshold of 0.3 instead of the default 0.5, which improved recall from near 0% to 85%. I evaluated using ROC-AUC and PR-AUC rather than accuracy, which would be misleading on imbalanced data."</p>
<hr class="border-border-200 border-t-0.5 my-3 mx-1.5">
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal"><strong>Q4: Why rate limiting before ML?</strong></p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal">"Two reasons. First, ML inference is expensive — running it on every request including abuse traffic wastes compute. Second, card-testing attacks involve hundreds of small transactions to find valid cards. Rate limiting blocks these deterministically in under 1ms, while ML would need to score each one individually. It's a cheap first line of defense that protects the expensive one."</p>
<hr class="border-border-200 border-t-0.5 my-3 mx-1.5">
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal"><strong>Q5: How does your graph detection work?</strong></p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal">"Every transaction adds three nodes to a graph — card, merchant, IP — and edges between them. When a new transaction arrives, I check how many different cards share the same IP and merchant. If 2+ cards share infrastructure, it signals a possible fraud ring. The graph risk score escalates progressively — 0.4 for 2 cards, 0.7 for 4+ cards sharing the same IP and merchant. This catches fraud rings that look innocent individually."</p>
<hr class="border-border-200 border-t-0.5 my-3 mx-1.5">
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal"><strong>Q6: What is the combined score formula and why?</strong></p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal">"Combined = 0.6 × ML + 0.4 × Graph. ML gets higher weight because it's trained on labeled data and is more precise. Graph gets 0.4 because it catches patterns ML misses — like multiple clean-looking cards sharing infrastructure. The ensemble catches more fraud than either model alone."</p>
<hr class="border-border-200 border-t-0.5 my-3 mx-1.5">
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal"><strong>Q7: How does Redis rate limiting differ from in-memory?</strong></p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal">"In-memory rate limiting only works for a single server instance — if you have 3 servers, each has its own counter and an attacker can send 5 requests to each. Redis is shared across all instances, so the limit is enforced globally. I use a sorted set with timestamps as members — on each request I remove old timestamps, count remaining, and add the new one atomically using a pipeline."</p>
<hr class="border-border-200 border-t-0.5 my-3 mx-1.5">
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal"><strong>Q8: How do you make fraud decisions explainable?</strong></p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal">"I return human-readable reasons with every decision — things like 'High transaction velocity in last 1 minute' or 'IP shared by 4 different cards — possible fraud ring'. This serves two purposes: compliance and audit trails, and customer dispute resolution. Banks legally need to explain why a transaction was blocked."</p>
<hr class="border-border-200 border-t-0.5 my-3 mx-1.5">
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal"><strong>Q9: What are your latency targets and how did you achieve them?</strong></p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal">"Rate limiter under 1ms, ML inference 4–10ms, end-to-end under 50ms P95. I achieved this by loading the model once at startup instead of per request, using pandas DataFrames with named columns to avoid schema validation overhead, and keeping the decision logic as simple conditionals rather than additional model calls."</p>
<hr class="border-border-200 border-t-0.5 my-3 mx-1.5">
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal"><strong>Q10: How would you scale this to production?</strong></p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal">"Three steps. First, make inference stateless so you can run multiple instances behind a load balancer — the model is loaded once per process. Second, Redis already handles distributed rate limiting across instances. Third, replace the in-memory graph with a persistent graph database like Neo4j for cross-restart fraud ring memory. For streaming, add Kafka in front to buffer transaction events and process them asynchronously."</p>
<hr class="border-border-200 border-t-0.5 my-3 mx-1.5">
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal"><strong>Q11: What happens if Redis goes down?</strong></p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal">"The system automatically falls back to in-memory rate limiting. I detect Redis availability at startup with a ping and set a flag. Every rate limit check uses Redis if available, otherwise falls back. This is graceful degradation — the system stays available but loses cross-instance coordination temporarily."</p>
<hr class="border-border-200 border-t-0.5 my-3 mx-1.5">
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal"><strong>Q12: How do you prevent data leakage in your ML model?</strong></p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal">"I use time-based splitting — first 80% of transactions by time for training, last 20% for testing. This prevents future information leaking into past predictions. All rolling window features (velocity counts) only look backwards in time. Random splitting would be wrong here because a transaction's neighbors in time would appear in both train and test sets."</p>
<hr class="border-border-200 border-t-0.5 my-3 mx-1.5">
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal"><strong>Q13: What would you add next?</strong></p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal">"Three things. First, Kafka streaming to ingest real-time transactions instead of API calls. Second, model monitoring to detect drift — if fraud patterns change, the model needs retraining. Third, a persistent graph database so fraud ring memory survives server restarts. For deployment, Docker and Kubernetes for horizontal scaling."</p>
<hr class="border-border-200 border-t-0.5 my-3 mx-1.5">
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal"><strong>Q14: How do you monitor this system in production?</strong></p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal">"Every request is logged as structured JSON with a unique request ID, timestamp, risk score, decision, and latency. The /health endpoint shows uptime, Redis status, and request counters. The /metrics endpoint shows fraud rate percentage, approval rate, and graph statistics. This lets ops teams spot anomalies without looking at individual logs."</p>
<hr class="border-border-200 border-t-0.5 my-3 mx-1.5">
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal"><strong>Q15: Why is this beyond a typical ML project?</strong></p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal">"Most ML projects stop at training a model and measuring accuracy. This system adds five production concerns that are missing from typical projects — abuse prevention via rate limiting, real-time inference as a service, graph-based pattern detection across transactions, explainable decisions for compliance, and observability via structured logging and metrics endpoints. It mirrors how real fintech fraud systems are actually built."</p>
<hr class="border-border-200 border-t-0.5 my-3 mx-1.5">
