an><span class="token token" style="color: rgb(0, 128, 128);">0.6</span><span> </span><span class="token token" style="color: rgb(20, 24, 31);">*</span><span> ml_score </span><span class="token token" style="color: rgb(20, 24, 31);">+</span><span> </span><span class="token token" style="color: rgb(0, 128, 128);">0.4</span><span> </span><span class="token token" style="color: rgb(20, 24, 31);">*</span><span> graph_score</span></span></code></pre></div></div>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal">Why 60/40?</p>
<ul class="[li_&amp;]:mb-0 [li_&amp;]:mt-1 [li_&amp;]:gap-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-disc flex flex-col gap-1 pl-8 mb-3">
<li class="font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">ML is trained on labeled data → more reliable → higher weight</li>
<li class="font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">Graph catches patterns ML misses → still important → 40% weight</li>
<li class="font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">Together they catch more than either alone</li>
</ul>
<hr class="border-border-200 border-t-0.5 my-3 mx-1.5">
<h3 class="text-text-100 mt-3 -mb-1 text-[1.125rem] font-bold">Interview Answer for Phase 4</h3>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal"><em>"I implemented graph-based fraud detection using NetworkX. Every transaction adds card, merchant, and IP as nodes with edges between them. When a new transaction arrives, I check how many cards share the same IP — two cards sharing infrastructure raises the graph risk score, and four or more triggers a fraud ring alert. The graph risk escalates progressively as the ring builds, and I combine it with the ML score in a 60/40 weighted ensemble. This catches fraud rings that look innocent to ML individually."</em></p>
<hr class="border-border-200 border-t-0.5 my-3 mx-1.5">
<hr class="border-border-200 border-t-0.5 my-3 mx-1.5">
