</span><span>
</span></span><span><span></span><span class="token token" style="color: rgb(0, 81, 194);">fetch</span><span class="token token" style="color: rgb(43, 48, 59);">(</span><span class="token token" style="color: rgb(0, 128, 0);">"https://bank.com/api/transfer?amount=50000&amp;to=hacker"</span><span class="token token" style="color: rgb(43, 48, 59);">)</span><span>
</span></span><span><span></span><span class="token token" style="color: rgb(110, 118, 135);">// Your browser is logged into bank.com</span><span>
</span></span><span><span></span><span class="token token" style="color: rgb(110, 118, 135);">// This request carries your bank session cookie</span><span>
</span></span><span><span></span><span class="token token" style="color: rgb(110, 118, 135);">// Money transferred without your knowledge!</span></span></code></pre></div></div>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal">This is called <strong>Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)</strong>. Browsers prevent this by blocking cross-origin requests by default.</p>
<hr class="border-border-200 border-t-0.5 my-3 mx-1.5">
