But "great" depends on where you're applying.
What Makes It Stand Out Right Now</p><ul class="[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-disc space-y-2.5 pl-7"><li class="whitespace-normal break-words">4 defensive layers in one system — most freshers build one</li><li class="whitespace-normal break-words">Live deployment with a URL you can put on your resume</li><li class="whitespace-normal break-words">Real numbers — ROC-AUC 0.9883, 16,215 req/min, 38.7ms P95, load tested, chaos tested</li><li class="whitespace-normal break-words">You know the weaknesses — interviewers love candidates who can critique their own work</li><li class="whitespace-normal break-words"><p class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Business context — you understand the ₹10,999 vs ₹900 cost tradeoff, not just accuracy metrics
Honest Assessment by Company
Company Is the project enough? What they'll actually test Razorpay ✅ Yes, strong fit Payments domain knowledge, system design Flipkart ✅ Yes DSA first, then project as bonus Stripe ⚠️ Strong but needs polish Deep system design, distributed systems thinking Google ⚠️ Project won't matter much DSA/algorithms is 80% of the bar
The One Thing That Actually Matters Now
The project is done. The gap is not code — it's you.
If an interviewer asks "walk me through your system" and you take 8 minutes and stumble on follow-up questions, the project won't save you. If you answer it in 90 seconds with confidence and correctly anticipate their next 3 questions, you'll stand out regardless of what's in the code.
Phase 10 (interview prep) has more ROI than any code improvement at this point.
If You Still Want to Add Something
The only addition that would genuinely impress interviewers at Stripe/Google level is one of these:</p></li><li class="whitespace-normal break-words">A/B testing framework — show you know how to deploy model versions safely</li><li class="whitespace-normal break-words">Prometheus + Grafana — production observability, shows real-world awareness</li><li class="whitespace-normal break-words">Card-level rate limiting — closes the IP rotation gap you already know exists
But do these after you're comfortable answering all 10 interview questions cold. Code improvements with weak answers = rejection. Solid answers with current code = offers.
My recommendation: Fix <code class="bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">threshold_optimizer.py</code> today (15 min), then spend the next 3–4 days on Phase 10. You're closer to interview-ready than you think — the gap is practice, not code.
