<hr class="border-border-200 border-t-0.5 my-3 mx-1.5">
<h3 class="text-text-100 mt-3 -mb-1 text-[1.125rem] font-bold">The Problem</h3>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal">creditcard.csv has no country/city. But we can <strong>infer geographic risk from the V-features</strong> themselves.</p>
<hr class="border-border-200 border-t-0.5 my-3 mx-1.5">
<h3 class="text-text-100 mt-3 -mb-1 text-[1.125rem] font-bold">The Insight</h3>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal">Remember V1-V28 are PCA of real bank features which include:</p>
<ul class="[li_&amp;]:mb-0 [li_&amp;]:mt-1 [li_&amp;]:gap-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-disc flex flex-col gap-1 pl-8 mb-3">
<li class="font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">Merchant country</li>
<li class="font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">Card issuing country</li>
<li class="font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">IP geolocation</li>
<li class="font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">Device location</li>
</ul>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal">So <strong>V-features already encode geographic information</strong> — just scrambled by PCA.</p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal">We can reverse-engineer which V-features correlate with geographic fraud by analyzing the actual fraud cases.</p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal">Run this to find out:</p>
