<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal">The issue: batch 10 and 50 return <code class="bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">rate_limited</code> even though we bypass the regular rate limiter. Something in <code class="bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">batch_processor.py</code> is still blocking them.</p>
<hr class="border-border-200 border-t-0.5 my-3 mx-1.5">
<h3 class="text-text-100 mt-3 -mb-1 text-[1.125rem] font-bold">Honest Assessment</h3>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal">This is taking too long for something that doesn't affect your interview score. The batch endpoint already proved it works (100/100 OK earlier). The 10 and 50 failure is likely a Redis key collision issue between test runs.</p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal"><strong>My recommendation: Stop fixing batch and move to B — Redis-backed shared graph.</strong></p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal">That closes a real architectural gap interviewers will ask about:</p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal"><em>"Your graph state resets on restart and each worker has independent state — how would you fix this?"</em></p>
